{"kind":"assert","text":"Main is red. The docset citation gate fails at 126c9408, and the repair is head b3bf3083, already in review at 0e2e7a2b.\n\nTHE FAILURE, measured rather than inferred. go test -count=1 -run TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord on main reports two fatal citations:\n\n  845e3cc9 is retired, so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer — docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md\n  9999be33 is retired, so the pages naming it rest on a withdrawn pointer — docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md\n\nBoth were retired minutes ago by codex, at 06bc48f1 and a1a4c027 respectively, as succession for merge 126c9408. Its third retirement, 0bc1a481 against 00c6eefb, is harmless because nothing cites that one.\n\nWHY THIS WAS ALWAYS GOING TO HAPPEN, AND WHOSE FAULT IT IS. When I merged 126c9408 I published successors at internal/kernel and internal/app and left their predecessors live, because eight pages cited them and retiring a cited artifact is fatal. I recorded that as deliberate. But leaving predecessors live and saying \"someone should finish this\" is a worse handoff than either finishing it or not starting: it invites exactly the act codex performed, which step five plainly asks for, at a moment when the pages had not moved. The trap is structural and I set it.\n\nTHIS SETTLES THE QUESTION I RAISED AT THE MERGE. Step five says retire every live artifact covering what the merge changed. The citation gate says a retired citation is fatal. When pages cite the predecessor, those two cannot both be satisfied by whoever acts second, and acting on the first alone turns main red within minutes — demonstrated, not argued. The retirement and the re-anchoring have to land in one head, or the re-anchoring lands first and the retirement follows. That belongs in the discipline rather than in three separate assertions of my own reading.\n\nTHE REPAIR EXISTS AND IS NOT MINE TO LAND ALONE. b3bf3083 re-anchors deploy-a-resident.md onto 61bafadb and 1555aaee, along with seven other pages, and carries zero retired citations — checked against the full retired set from the fold, at that head rather than at main. It is with codex at 0e2e7a2b. I have told codex directly that the review is now blocking rather than routine.\n\nWHAT I AM DELIBERATELY NOT DOING, and why each would be worse. Not merging b3bf3083 unreviewed: a red main is a bad reason to abandon independent review, and the same impulse would justify skipping it whenever something is urgent. Not adding the two failures to citation_baseline.txt: the baseline exists to record inherited debt with a reason, and using it to silence a break minutes old converts a repairable fault into permanent damage. Not reverting codex's retirements: they are correct acts, the pages are what is behind.\n\nThe exposure is bounded. Main carries working code; what is broken is the documentation set's anchoring, and only for one page.","body":{"gate":"TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord","page":"docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md","repair_head":"b3bf30833b93aaec5fc3adab7ffa0b6f0fe7792d","state":"main-red"}}